From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-gpu, cocoa: the guest's vblanks follow the Mac's refresh

What: a guest that asks (OmacVM's virtio-gpu driver with vsync_lock=1,
src/app/guest/virtio-gpu/linux-virtio-gpu-vsync-lock.patch) gets the
main window display's refresh grid through OmacVM's own virtio-gpu
command (0x0f00, answer 0x11f0): the guest counter value of the next
vblank it should have and the period in ps. Its vblank timer follows
that grid. The grid: while the guest asked in the last 2 s, each tick of
the present's display link puts the guest's vblank the lead before that
refresh's commit (the commit is 3 ms before the vsync,
qemu-cocoa-gl-present-vsync.patch), at the full rate's period measured
over the link's run of ticks. The lead follows when the guest's frames
are ready after their vblank (ui/omacvm-vsync.h: the latest of the last
64 + 1 ms, once a second; OMACVM_GL_FOLLOW_LEAD_MS fixes it for tests).
A frame later than the lead misses its refresh, so the lead covers the
slowest one, not a percentile: the first build (95th percentile + 1 ms)
had 1-3 late frames a second at 60 Hz, each a repeat. While a
guest follows, a tick that finds more than one frame shows the newest:
the older one came late for its own refresh (as OMACVM_GL_INPUT_FIRST
does while input comes), so a late frame costs one repeat, not a
refresh of delay until the quarter-second trim. hvf gives the guest
counter's offset (CNTVCT = mach time less hvf's vtimer offset, set at
start and after each pause); ui/omacvm-vsync.c keeps the grid between the
threads (a spin lock). Another scanout, no grid yet (the link has not
ticked), the 2D device or OMACVM_GL_VSYNC=0: the answer says no grid and
the guest's timer runs on its own. A guest that never asks changes
nothing.

Why: the guest's vblank timer runs at the display's rate but at its own
phase. Hyprland draws right after a vblank and QEMU shows the frame at the
next refresh it is in time for, so while frames come steadily (the pointer
moving, an animation, a video) each frame waited anywhere up to a refresh
on the Mac for nothing: a key while the pointer moves took about 11 ms
more than on an idle screen at 60 Hz (src/tests/input-latency-vm.sh, Mac
mini, 3.2.0), where a native app waits only for its own next refresh.

Threads: the commit queue (present_lock held) publishes the grid and
reads when the guest asked; QEMU's present thread adds the arrivals under
present_lock; virtio-gpu (main loop, BQL) answers under the store's spin
lock; hvf writes the offset under it. Trace cocoa_present_follow once a
second: the lead, the frames' median and latest after their vblank, how
many came late.

Upstream: OmacVM only (its present and its own virtio-gpu command).
Test: app/runtime/Tests/display/test-vsync-follow.sh (rules, wiring);
src/tests/virtio-gpu-vsync.sh (the guest's side); end to end
src/tests/input-latency-vm.sh --smbios omacvm.vsynclock=1.
Applies after omacvm-cocoa-no-app-nap.patch and omacvm-vsync-follow-logic.patch.

--- a/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
+++ b/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@
 #include "hw/virtio/virtio-gpu-bswap.h"
 #include "hw/virtio/virtio-gpu-pixman.h"
 #include "system/hvf.h"
+#include "ui/omacvm-vsync.h"
 
 #include "ui/egl-helpers.h"
 
@@ -556,6 +557,45 @@ static void virtio_gpu_rect_update(VirtIOGPU *g, int idx, int x, int y,
     qemu_console_gl_update(g->parent_obj.scanout[idx].con, x, y, width, height);
 }
 
+/*
+ * OmacVM: the Mac display's refresh for the guest's vblank timer (OmacVM's
+ * virtio-gpu driver with vsync_lock=1, ui/omacvm-vsync.h). The answer has
+ * the guest counter of the next vblank the guest should have and the
+ * period; no grid (the main window's display link has not run yet, another
+ * scanout): VALID clear, and the guest's timer runs on its own.
+ */
+struct omacvm_virtio_gpu_vsync {
+    struct virtio_gpu_ctrl_hdr hdr;
+    uint32_t scanout_id;
+    uint32_t padding;
+};
+
+struct omacvm_virtio_gpu_resp_vsync {
+    struct virtio_gpu_ctrl_hdr hdr;
+    uint64_t vblank;
+    uint64_t period_ps;
+    uint32_t flags;
+    uint32_t padding;
+};
+
+static void virgl_cmd_omacvm_vsync(VirtIOGPU *g,
+                                   struct virtio_gpu_ctrl_command *cmd)
+{
+    struct omacvm_virtio_gpu_vsync req;
+    struct omacvm_virtio_gpu_resp_vsync resp = { 0 };
+    uint64_t counter, period_ps;
+
+    VIRTIO_GPU_FILL_CMD(req);
+    resp.hdr.type = OMACVM_VIRTIO_GPU_RESP_OK_VSYNC;   /* swapped with the header */
+    if (le32_to_cpu(req.scanout_id) == 0 &&
+        omacvm_vsync_for_guest(&counter, &period_ps)) {
+        resp.vblank = cpu_to_le64(counter);
+        resp.period_ps = cpu_to_le64(period_ps);
+        resp.flags = cpu_to_le32(OMACVM_VSYNC_VALID);
+    }
+    virtio_gpu_ctrl_response(g, cmd, &resp.hdr, sizeof(resp));
+}
+
 static void virgl_cmd_resource_flush(VirtIOGPU *g,
                                      struct virtio_gpu_ctrl_command *cmd)
 {
@@ -1229,6 +1269,9 @@ void virtio_gpu_virgl_process_cmd(VirtIOGPU *g,
     case VIRTIO_GPU_CMD_GET_EDID:
         virtio_gpu_get_edid(g, cmd);
         break;
+    case OMACVM_VIRTIO_GPU_CMD_VSYNC:
+        virgl_cmd_omacvm_vsync(g, cmd);
+        break;
 #if VIRGL_VERSION_MAJOR >= 1
     case VIRTIO_GPU_CMD_RESOURCE_CREATE_BLOB:
         virgl_cmd_resource_create_blob(g, cmd);
